{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"2682 CLOSED with both attestations — runner.py 800->655, structured_run.py 178, ZERO test edits (runner imports the moved callables by name so all 7 reference sites, including 3 string-literal monkeypatch targets, still resolve). Full suite 7524 = 7517 baseline + 7 seam tests, change 1032 +1/+1. Stack now 4 deep. Verified the TYPE_CHECKING guard is load-bearing: mutating it to a runtime import yields a real partially-initialized-module ImportError. S3 (2932) plan-review PASS after 7 passes (10->1->3->6->1->0 blocking). The last two findings were ones I could NOT have reasoned out unaided: (a) I promised a 400-to-actionable-error translation with no catch site, predicate or output — now specified from the MEASURED error strings (three required conjuncts: 400/ValidationException AND a sampling-param name AND a rejection word, so unrelated 400s are not swallowed), living in the provider-agnostic failure.py rather than the Bedrock leaf so no provider-name branch enters runner.py; (b) I named tests/live/ for the canary, which DOES NOT EXIST — the repo puts env-gated billable tests in tests/external/ and conftest.py structurally auto-marks that dir external AND hard-fails collection for anything marked external outside it, so tests/live/ would either have made billable AWS calls on every default CI run or broken collection for the whole suite. Also added a SEQUENCING edge 2932 blocks 343b: S5 stamps the WHOLE ModelCapabilities record into a SIGNED payload and S3 adds supports_temperature to it, so landing S5 first would change an already-signed shape — the same argument that kept supports_thinking on the record. S3 CLAIMED."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785402575477546001,"uuid":"f17ba7e4-7bf2-4c7a-a363-18c8497eafbc"}